.TH CVEC_CONSTRUCT_MP_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_construct_mp_scoped \- construct a vector with a custom allocator, destroyed automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_construct_mp_scoped(v, type, mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a vector variable named v that holds elements of the
given type, creates the vector through mprocs, and adds a cleanup handler
that destroys the vector when control leaves the scope around it. The
macro is cvec_construct_mp(3) plus that handler, with one more
difference: this macro initializes the companion type variables to NULL,
while cvec_construct_mp(3) deliberately leaves them indeterminate.
Everything else that cvec_construct_mp(3) documents holds here unchanged,
including the private copy of the procedure table that the vector keeps
and the stop of the program through ccol_fatal_err() for a failed
creation.

The macro initializes the variable to NULL before it creates the vector,
which makes the handler safe on a path that leaves the block between the
declaration and a successful creation. The destruction happens on every
exit from the scope: the closing brace, a return, a break, a continue, or
a goto out of the block. Because the macro sets the variable to NULL as
it destroys the vector, an explicit call to cvec_destroy(3) earlier in
the block frees nothing twice.

The vector goes back through the same procedures that created it, so
those functions must be callable at the end of the scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v       Name of the vector variable to declare and create
type    Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
mprocs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t. Its malloc, calloc, realloc
        and free members must all be set. Pass NULL for the default
        allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to declarations and then a statement, not to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when it cannot
create the vector, with a message that names v and the error below it.
The macro is built on the cleanup attribute, so it needs GCC or Clang.
On a toolchain that does not have that attribute, use
cvec_construct_mp(3) with an explicit cvec_destroy(3).
Because the macro expands to declarations and then a statement, it
belongs where declarations are legal and cannot be the body of an if or
a loop without braces.
The macro pastes v into the names of the companion variables, so v must
be a plain identifier.
When the vector holds pointers to heap buffers, the macro does not free
the elements one by one, so free the element data before you leave the
scope.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The vector must not live longer than its scope: do not return v, and do
not store it anywhere that code reads after the end of the block. When
the ownership must leave the block, use cvec_construct_mp(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *procs) {
    cvec_construct_mp_scoped(vec, int, procs);

    cvec_push(vec, 42);

    if (cvec_size(vec) != 1) {
        return;   /* vec is destroyed here */
    }
}                 /* and here */
.fi
